  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "bs-05-mixed-upstream-downstream-systems",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Mixed Upstream–Downstream Systems",
    "description": "Solve two mixed journey equations for upstream and downstream speeds, then recover still-water speed and current.",
    "teachingApproach": "Show first that the awkward equations are linear in the reciprocals of the two effective speeds. Use short elimination with p=1/U and q=1/D, then convert back to speeds. Scale up to swapped-distance systems, linked towns, and two boats sharing one current.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 19, Boats and Streams. Approved scope: Teach the chapter’s two-equation pattern where one journey mixes upstream and downstream distances and a second journey gives another mix. Introduce p = 1/(upstream speed) and q = 1/(downstream speed) when that turns reciprocal equations into a simple linear system. Cover swapped-distance systems, extracting still-water/current speeds after solving the two effective rates, equidistant three-town river problems, and two-boat problems that share the same current. Keep algebra readable and verify recovered rates by replaying one original trip."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the hardest-looking pattern in this chapter without drowning in fractions.\n\nWhen two journeys mix upstream and downstream distances, the unknowns often become simple if we solve for the reciprocals of the two effective speeds.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose:\n40 km upstream + 55 km downstream takes 13 h,\n30 km upstream + 44 km downstream takes 10 h.\n\nLet U be upstream speed and D downstream speed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The equations are:\n40/U + 55/D = 13\n30/U + 44/D = 10.\n\nSet p=1/U and q=1/D. Now they are linear:\n40p+55q=13\n30p+44q=10.\nSolving gives U=5, D=11, so still water=8 and current=3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "For the mixed journeys 40/U + 55/D = 13 and 30/U + 44/D = 10, the effective speeds are U=5 and D=11 km/hr. What is the still-water speed?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "8 km/hr"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "3 km/hr"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "6 km/hr"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "16 km/hr"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
        "mode": "INLINE",
        "inlineData": {
          "questionType": "PRACTICE",
